// Class B, and the file that says why the two classes are ordered rather than
// parallel: this shape has BOTH faces at once.
//
// `fmt` is never called, and its body calls `push_int` rather than `push_str`.
// The reference emits `#include <string.h>` for the named builder - the class-A
// face - AND keeps the call while dropping the callee, so its own build fails on
// an undefined `StringBuilder__push_int`. Only the second of those decides where
// the file lives: a fixture whose reference output does not LINK belongs to
// `dropped_`, whatever else is also true of it.
//
// Filed the other way it is not merely mis-sorted, it is red: the port defines
// the whole `push_int` chain the reference dropped, so the class-A diff carries
// sixty-one lines the reference does not have, and the one-way assertion fires.
// That is this file's second job - it is the proof of an arm no other fixture
// reaches, because a program that uses a string makes the divergence vanish
// before either side can show it.
//
// So #71 is not about `push_str`. The property is: any prelude method called
// from a method nobody calls.
struct P { x: int }

impl Display for P {
    fn fmt(*const self, out: *StringBuilder) { out.push_int(self.x); }
}

fn main() {
    let p = P { x: 5 };
    println(p.x);
}
